::heading level=1 id=title
Self-Host — index, design, docs, goals (the one document)
::end

::paragraph id=status
Revision 4, 2026-09-19. This is the one design document — `docs/architecture.dx` was folded in and deleted (rule 7: one copy); its round-by-round history now lives in `docs/labs/redesign-history.dx`. Where code disagrees with the design, the code is wrong. Agents: read this first, use only its words, tick `now` as you finish, and run the gates in section 7 instead of ad-hoc shell. Keep it short — detail belongs in the linked labs.
::end

::heading level=2 id=charter
1. What it is
::end

::paragraph id=charter-body
**We are the vendor.** A general-purpose server for anything, on our own hardware: public website, public Minecraft server, database, a client's site, a locked-down admin panel. Connect a repo → it builds → it goes live → its owner chooses who reaches it. Used equally by people and by their agents. Everything in the chain is ours (Rust; all UI in RUI). It is the platform an autonomous software company runs on.
::end

::code id=flow-diagram lang=text
repo --push--> BUILD --> DEPLOY --> running THING --> EXPOSURE
                                                       |- public  : open to the internet (domain or raw TCP/UDP port) — the normal case
                                                       |- people  : must sign in and hold a Grant
                                                       '- private : people + must arrive through the VPN
::end

::heading level=2 id=nouns
2. The words (use only these)
::end

::bulleted-list id=nouns-list
- **Person** — a human who can sign in; has an email. One registry (`crates/foundation/identity`). Never "user"/"account".
- **Owner** — not a separate identity: a Person holding the `owner` Grant, which implies every other Grant. A deployment may have several. First-run setup creates the first.
- **Agent** — a token that acts for one Person and can never exceed that Person's Grants. Revoking the Person revokes their Agents.
- **Grant** — a power a Person holds (`owner`, `site.access:<site>`, `site.admin:<site>`, `service.control` …). Never "permission"/"role".
- **Site** — a web domain the proxy answers for. Has an owner and an Exposure.
- **Port** — a raw TCP/UDP service opened to the internet. Has an owner and an Exposure.
- **Service** — a process a Person chose to host. Parts of Self-Host itself (proxy, relay, mail, DNS, updater) are **System**: shown on the health screen, never listed as Services.
- **Deploy** — one repo→build→publish run, with a log and a recorded result.
- **Peer** — one device enrolled in the VPN; belongs to exactly one Person.
- **Pass** — short-lived daemon-signed token naming a Person; what the proxy checks.
::end

::heading level=2 id=rules
3. Rules
::end

::numbered-list id=rules-list
- **One store.** Every noun is an atomic record in our own embedded database crate: transactional, audited, applied live. No config file. Secrets in the keychain. Export/import is a snapshot only.
- **One writer, four faces.** Only the admin API writes. Web console, native console, CLI and MCP expose the same operations.
- **One login, one decision.** One sign-in for console, gated Sites and the VPN app. Every "may they?" question — site, relay, console, agent — goes through `Policy::decide`; nothing checks Grants on its own. An Owner therefore reaches everything without granting themselves anything.
- **Identity is a signed Pass, not a network position.** No per-person ports, no trusting a subnet. The relay verifies the Peer key and stamps the Person in-process.
- **Owners delegate.** A Site's owner grants access to their Site. Nothing is hard-coded to one person or to the console.
- **Agents act for a Person, behind the VPN.** An Agent belongs to one registered Person and is capped by that Person's current Grants. No public agent door. Observability is Deploy records and logs through the API/MCP. SSH is break-glass. `selfhost home mcp` is a separate MCP server by design (different domain).
- **One system per concept, one copy of the code.** A replaced system is deleted in the same change. One checkout is canonical; worktrees are temporary.
- **An update may never take the box down.** The updater builds first, swaps, restarts through the one registered start mechanism (`selfhost-daemon` task on Windows), health-checks, and rolls back automatically on failure. Every release gets a real Windows build before `main`.
- Control never depends on what it controls. The recovery path (VPN-to-SSH relay, watchdog, last-known-good binary) is its own small process with its own pinned device key; it does not read the People registry, does not share the daemon's binary, and is never restarted by a deploy. The owner can always repair identity locally on the box with no daemon running (`selfhost init-owner`, `selfhost people`).
- Nothing reaches `main` until that exact commit has been built AND has started and stayed healthy on the production OS (Windows), run by hand from a separate folder beside the live install. Mac-side checks (`cargo check --target …windows-gnu`, tests) prove compilation only — two outages on 2026-09-19 came from trusting them.
::end

::heading level=2 id=map
4. Where things are
::end

::bulleted-list id=map-list
- Layers: `crates/foundation` → `net` → `services` → `ui` → `app`. Build order is the dependency order.
- Identity/Grants/Policy/Pass: `crates/foundation/identity`. Login/sessions: `crates/foundation/login`.
- Admin API (loopback :9191, the only writer): `crates/app/admin`. Proxy 80/443 + Pass gate: `crates/app/proxy`. CLI + MCP server + self-update: `crates/app/cli`.
- VPN relay runner/enrolment: `crates/services/vpn`. VPN app: `crates/ui/vpn-ui`. Native console: `crates/ui/console`. UI library: `crates/ui/rui`.
- Web: `sites/console`, `sites/auth`.
- Security guidebook (mandatory before networked code): `docs/SECURITY.md`. Labs (detail, transcripts): `docs/labs/*.dx`. Incidents: `docs/incidents/`.
- Production: ALEX-DESKTOP (Windows, LAN 192.168.1.8, repo `C:\Users\Alex\Self-Host`, data `.\data`, task `selfhost-daemon`). Push to `main` = deploy.
::end

::heading level=2 id=gaps
5. Where the code disagrees today
::end

::bulleted-list id=gaps-list
- **Owner-as-Grant closed, 2026-09-19** (`round3/owner-person`): `Capability::Owner` is now a held Grant, not only `Identity::Owner`'s console password; VPN enrolment and Site sign-in route through `Policy::decide` instead of a direct Grant check, closing the "Owner refused on their own VPN" bug. Still open: `selfhost init-owner` is not yet wired into a true one-command first-run, and `Identity::Owner`/the console password itself still exists as a parallel credential — deleting it is unstarted. Rule 3 is now met for the read path; the identity is not yet gone.
- **Updater build-first/rollback closed, 2026-09-19** (`round3/safe-updater`): `crates/app/cli/src/self_update.rs` builds first in place, keeps a canonical `selfhost.prev`, marks `self-update.pending`, health-checks after restart with the same `probe_all` `selfhost health` uses, and auto-rolls-back (binary + git reset) on failure; regression-tested. Still open: no real Windows build gate runs before a merge to `main` — this repository has no CI configuration for one to live in. Rule 8's runtime half is met; the pre-merge half is not.
- **Redesign not live:** steps 1–4 exist on `redesign/round-2`; `main` is the pre-redesign code.
- **Console has no UI for Exposure / owner / per-site Grants.** `crates/ui/console/src/view/sites.rs` (mirrored in `sites/console`) ships a read-only SITES screen — name, domains, exposure, owner — with no control to change any of them; that stays admin-API/CLI/MCP-only. (CLI `people` writing through the admin API first, file only as a bootstrap fallback, is done — closed by `round2/cli-people`, see `docs/labs/redesign-history.dx`.)
- State still in ~8 files, not the store. Two deploy code paths (`selfhost_git`, `selfhost_app_deploy`).
- Not yet ours: Python Secure-VPN, tokio, rustls, ring, rcgen, serde, toml.
- Leaked GitHub token (old `com.engine.server.plist`) — **still live as of last check; must be revoked by Alex at github.com/settings/tokens.**
- **Rule 9 break-glass + watchdog landed, 2026-09-19** (`round4/break-glass`): `crates/app/cli/src/breakglass.rs` pins Ed25519 device keys in `<data_dir>/breakglass.keys`, independent of the People registry (`selfhost breakglass list|add|remove`). `crates/app/cli/src/watchdog.rs` is a pure state machine (`selfhost watchdog`) run from its own binary copy (`selfhost-watchdog`, `crates/app/cli/src/service_install.rs::watchdog_plan`/`install_watchdog_binary`, wired into `selfhost service install|uninstall` alongside, never touched by self-update) that restarts the registered daemon task, then restores `selfhost.prev`, then — if the exe is missing outright or still unhealthy — fetches/fast-forwards/rebuilds the deploy branch itself and installs atomically (reusing `self_update`'s `git`/`fast_forwards`/`install_atomically`/`built_binary_path`/`prev_path`/`restore_previous_binary`, now `pub(crate)`, not copied), restoring `selfhost.prev` first if it exists so something old is serving while the rebuild runs. Still open, deliberately out of scope this round: (1) the SSH relay's Windows Job Object (`Job::kill_on_drop` in `crates/foundation/supervisor/src/job.rs`) still ties the relay's life to the daemon's process — a real rule-9 violation the watchdog's task-restart papers over but does not fix; (2) `crates/services/vpn/src/runner.rs::plan()` still builds relay argv with `--account-manager`/`--account-manager-token-file`, so a completed handshake still needs a capability check against the daemon's own admin API — deeper than the Job Object issue, and untouched because it is byte-for-byte tested against the production argv and its server side lives in an external `server.py`; (3) mirrored break-glass `.pub` files are not yet wired into `runner::plan`'s `--peer` argv, so pinning a break-glass key alone does not yet make a running relay accept it. See `docs/labs/breakglass-watchdog.dx` for the full investigation.
::end

::heading level=2 id=plan
6. Goals, in order
::end

::checklist id=now
[x] 1. Delete the dead account system, deploy scripts, duplicate indexes
[x] 2. Access by identity: Exposure, Pass, proxy gate, Peer→Person, owner delegation (on branch)
[x] 3. Deploy records; System vs Service split (on branch)
[ ] 4. Round-2: MCP parity test, Agent→Person binding, relay→Site mapping, console UI for Exposure/Grants, CLI people through the API
[ ] 5. Owner is a Person with the `owner` Grant; every check through `Policy::decide`; one-command first-run; delete the console password
[ ] 6. Safe updater: build-first, swap, restart via the registered task, health-check, auto-rollback; real Windows build gate
[ ] 7. Deploy to ALEX-DESKTOP and live-test together (sign-in, VPN, a public Site, a people Site, a private Site)
[ ] 8. The store: database crate; import files once; delete file parsers
[ ] 9. Netlify screens in RUI: Sites, Deploys, Domains, Ports, People, System health
[ ] 10. Own the chain: Rust relay, then vendor/replace the rest, crypto last
[x] 11. Rule 9: break-glass SSH device keys + independent watchdog with build-from-source fallback (on branch, `round4/break-glass`); Job Object coupling and the relay's `--account-manager` dependency remain open
::end

